      "writtenOn": "2026-05-06T23:09:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This early return will allow the host to trigger executing `pkvm_vcpu_pvmfw_entry_init()` or `pkvm_vcpu_ap_entry_init()` more than once (since `kvm_vcpu_has_run()` is still false)? Although it _seems_ that wouldn\u0027t be harmful, since currently both `pkvm_vcpu_pvmfw_entry_init()` or `pkvm_vcpu_ap_entry_init()` implementations are \"idempotent\". But still it seems cleaner, and potentially safer, to avoid allowing that?\n\nFurthermore, we don\u0027t really need the CR3/PDPTR setup here, we only need to load the EPT?\n\nSo maybe we\u0027d better just call `kvm_x86_call(load_mmu_pgd)()` instead of `pkvm_load_mmu_pgd()` here?",
      "revId": "f393bebdd3097b25b477075b23e06795659a2efc",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c12180cb_70f11abf",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/pkvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 1645,
      "author": {
        "id": 4085392
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-08T03:14:22Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For the case booting with pvmfw, we don\u0027t need CR3/PDPTR. For the case without pvmfw, it at least still needs CR3 to directly booting from 64bit paging mode. But as pkvm_load_mmu_pgd is exposed to the host, the host can call this before running the vCPU to setup CR3/PDPTR for pVM without pvmfw.\n\nConsidering this, if the host doesn\u0027t use this PV interface, and the pKVM doesn\u0027t load the guest mmu before running cpu, the guest will not be able to run. However there are many ways for the host to block the guest to run, e.g., refuse to map in guest mmu. If we prefer to enforce the guest mmu loading, we can just do as you suggested to simplify.  so what is the advantage of having this code to enforce guest mmu loading?\n\nIf we prefer to enforce the guest mmu loading for some reason, maybe we just need to enforce this for pVM (either w/ pvmfw or w/o pvmfw) only?\n```\n                                pkvm_vcpu_pvmfw_entry_init(vcpu);\n                        else if (!kvm_vcpu_is_reset_bsp(vcpu))\n                                pkvm_vcpu_ap_entry_init(vcpu);\n+\n+                       kvm_x86_call(load_mmu_pgd)(vcpu, vcpu-\u003earch.mmu-\u003eroot.hpa,\n+                                                  vcpu-\u003earch.mmu-\u003eroot_role.level);\n                }\n        }\n\n-       if (unlikely(!kvm_vcpu_has_run(vcpu)))\n-               pkvm_load_mmu_pgd(vcpu);\n-\n```",
      "parentUuid": "31b42710_6d98737e",
      "revId": "f393bebdd3097b25b477075b23e06795659a2efc",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e7ac2b8e_0f4ef3d5",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/pkvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 1645,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-08T11:27:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Considering this, if the host doesn\u0027t use this PV interface, and the pKVM doesn\u0027t load the guest mmu before running cpu, the guest will not be able to run.\n\nIf the host wants CR3 to be set, the host needs to make the load_mmu_pgd call anyway? If it doesn\u0027t, it is a host\u0027s bug, not a pKVM\u0027s problem? Or am I missing something?\n\nActually I think KVM-high always makes sure to call kvm_mmu_load() -\u003e kvm_mmu_load_pgd() -\u003e pkvm_load_mmu_pgd() before running a vCPU first time, even if it doesn\u0027t need to set CR3 (e.g. even if it doesn\u0027t boot the VM directly from 64-bit mode)?\n\n\u003e If we prefer to enforce the guest mmu loading, we can just do as you suggested to simplify. so what is the advantage of having this code to enforce guest mmu loading?\n\nI\u0027m not sure what you mean. The reason for enforcing guest mmu loading is not to ensure that the guest will boot successfully from 64-bit mode (that is the host\u0027s concern, not pKVM\u0027s) but to ensure that if a vCPU runs, it always runs with the correct EPT loaded (so pKVM\u0027s memory protection is always correctly enforced).\n\n(Side note: all this is a bit confusing due to the fact that this KVM\u0027s load_mmu_pgd API is responsible for both EPT setup and CR3/PDPTR setup, which are quite different things; in particular in the pKVM case, the EPT setup is strictly for pKVM\u0027s needs, while the CR3/PDPTR setup is strictly for the host\u0027s needs.)\n\n\u003e If we prefer to enforce the guest mmu loading for some reason, maybe we just need to enforce this for pVM (either w/ pvmfw or w/o pvmfw) only?\n\nWe need to enforce loading correct EPT for npVM as well, otherwise pKVM has no control over which pages a npVM has access to?",
      "parentUuid": "c12180cb_70f11abf",
      "revId": "f393bebdd3097b25b477075b23e06795659a2efc",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c92773b9_57b4bc22",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/pkvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 1645,
      "author": {
        "id": 4085392
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-08T12:19:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e I\u0027m not sure what you mean. The reason for enforcing guest mmu loading is not to ensure that the guest will boot successfully from 64-bit mode (that is the host\u0027s concern, not pKVM\u0027s) but to ensure that if a vCPU runs, it always runs with the correct EPT loaded (so pKVM\u0027s memory protection is always correctly enforced).\n\nAs you pointed out \"Actually I think KVM-high always makes sure to call kvm_mmu_load() -\u003e kvm_mmu_load_pgd() -\u003e pkvm_load_mmu_pgd() before running a vCPU first time, even if it doesn\u0027t need to set CR3 (e.g. even if it doesn\u0027t boot the VM directly from 64-bit mode)?\", so the MMU is already loaded via PV interface before we reach here, and we don\u0027t have to load mmu again right here, right?\n\nAnd if we don\u0027t enforce mmu loading at here, and the host is maliciously not using PV interface to load mmu, then the guest\u0027s EPTP is not programmed, so the guest is not able to run.\n\nThat is why I asked what is the advantage of enforce guest mmu loading. I didn\u0027t understand your point that, \"ensure that if a vCPU runs, it always runs with the correct EPT loaded (so pKVM\u0027s memory protection is always correctly enforced).\", as my understanding is that a guest should not be able to run with EPTP not programmed.",
      "parentUuid": "e7ac2b8e_0f4ef3d5",
      "revId": "f393bebdd3097b25b477075b23e06795659a2efc",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "addc69eb_0e60733c",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/pkvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 1645,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-08T12:38:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ah ok, if it is indeed guaranteed that the guest will not run if EPTP is not programmed, maybe we don\u0027t need to enforce it...\n\nDoes the SDM guarantee that? What exactly happens when we execute VMLAUNCH/VMRESUME without programming EPT_POINTER before that?\n\nI was thinking what happens is: the HW uses some initial (undefined?) value of EPT_POINTER, and then, _maybe_ most likely it will fail since this value will be malformed, but it is not guaranteed (i.e. it may be accidentally a valid EPTP value)?",
      "parentUuid": "c92773b9_57b4bc22",
      "revId": "f393bebdd3097b25b477075b23e06795659a2efc",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "14c4902b_5fcda3ce",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/pkvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 1645,
      "author": {
        "id": 4085392
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-08T13:00:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The EPTP will be checked before enter guest, SDM mentioned:\n```\nIf the “enable EPT” VM-execution control is 1, the EPTP VM-execution control field (see Table 26-9 in Section\n26.6.11) must satisfy the following checks:\n— The EPT memory type (bits 2:0) must be a value supported by the processor as indicated in the\nIA32_VMX_EPT_VPID_CAP MSR (see Appendix A.10).\n— Bits 5:3 must contain a value 1 less than an EPT page-walk length supported by the processor as indicated\nin the IA32_VMX_EPT_VPID_CAP MSR (see Section 30.3.2 and Appendix A.10).\n— Bit 6 (enable bit for accessed and dirty flags for EPT) must be 0 if bit 21 of the IA32_VMX_EPT_VPID_CAP\nMSR (see Appendix A.10) is read as 0, indicating that the processor does not support accessed and dirty\nflags for EPT.\n— Reserved bits 11:7 and 63:N (where N is the processor’s physical-address width) must all be 0.\n```\n\nBit5:3 should be 1 less than the actual EPT page table level, so it should be 3 or 4. If we don\u0027t program EPTP, it is 0, which should be failed for the above check.",
      "parentUuid": "addc69eb_0e60733c",
      "revId": "f393bebdd3097b25b477075b23e06795659a2efc",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4fbc7988_f119f5db",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/pkvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 1645,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-08T13:10:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e If we don\u0027t program EPTP, it is 0\n\nIs that guaranteed?\n\nBTW is that guaranteed for all other VMCS fields as well (i.e. are they all zero-initialized by HW)?",
      "parentUuid": "14c4902b_5fcda3ce",
      "revId": "f393bebdd3097b25b477075b23e06795659a2efc",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fc72bcb7_6706547d",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/pkvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 1645,
      "author": {
        "id": 4085392
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-09T01:14:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We donate the VMCS memory page from the host to the pKVM via `if (pkvm_host_donate_hyp(__pkvm_pa(loaded_vmcs-\u003evmcs), PAGE_SIZE, true))` in vmx.c when creating vcpu with passing true to clear the entire page. So the EPTP field should be zero if we never program it.\n\nAn experiment I do is not to write EPTP VMCS field. And the guest is failed to run. the VMCS reading of EPTP shows it is 0.\n\nNow I got your point about the purpose of the enforcement, to make sure not to leave EPTP with some unexpected value. So I agree this is useful, which can prevent some potential issue caused by programming EPTP unexpected with some meaningful value. So yeah, if we want to enforce mmu loading, then we should enforce for both npVM and pVM. In this case, we can directly call `kvm_x86_call(load_mmu_pgd)` rather than `pkvm_load_mmu_pgd`",
      "parentUuid": "4fbc7988_f119f5db",
      "revId": "f393bebdd3097b25b477075b23e06795659a2efc",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5157eb11_de39b8bb",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/pkvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 1645,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-09T09:19:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e We donate the VMCS memory page from the host to the pKVM via `if (pkvm_host_donate_hyp(__pkvm_pa(loaded_vmcs-\u003evmcs), PAGE_SIZE, true))` in vmx.c when creating vcpu with passing true to clear the entire page. So the EPTP field should be zero if we never program it.\n\nAh, so the HW guarantees that the initial values of VMCS fields will be the values stored in the VMCS memory page at the moment of the VMPTRLD instruction? If so, it makes sense, but do you know if there is an explicit statement in the SDM about that?\n\n\u003e Now I got your point about the purpose of the enforcement, to make sure not to leave EPTP with some unexpected value. So I agree this is useful, which can prevent some potential issue caused by programming EPTP unexpected with some meaningful value.\n\nYeah, what I\u0027m thinking is: if we want to rely on the fact that the initial EPTP value will be 0 which _happens_ to be an invalid EPTP value (due to page table level bits set to 0), we\u0027d better add a comment explaining that. But indeed it might be more useful to enforce loading correct EPT here. Also it \"conceptually\" makes sense: it is the pKVM hypervisor that is responsible for the EPT, and it doesn\u0027t need the host\u0027s help to know which EPT should be loaded and when.",
      "parentUuid": "fc72bcb7_6706547d",
      "revId": "f393bebdd3097b25b477075b23e06795659a2efc",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3a8f92eb_c00f3ec0",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/pkvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 1645,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-09T09:30:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Ah, so the HW guarantees that the initial values of VMCS fields will be the values stored in the VMCS memory page at the moment of the VMPTRLD instruction? If so, it makes sense, but do you know if there is an explicit statement in the SDM about that?\n\nAnd if so, do we need to clflush the VMCS page before the first VMPTRLD if we want to rely on that? :)",
      "parentUuid": "5157eb11_de39b8bb",
      "revId": "f393bebdd3097b25b477075b23e06795659a2efc",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "48758e5c_56de6fa6",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/pkvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 1645,
      "author": {
        "id": 4085392
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-09T09:58:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Actually according to SDM, no VMWRITE to required VMCS field will result in unpredictable behaviors, which implicitly mean this is determined by the microarchitecture implementations.",
      "parentUuid": "3a8f92eb_c00f3ec0",
      "revId": "f393bebdd3097b25b477075b23e06795659a2efc",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2b498279_998e3f68",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/pkvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 1645,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-09T10:09:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I see. So seems like we indeed should enforce load_mmu_pgd here. (Or alternatively we could check if the host has loaded it via the PV call, and return an error otherwise. The \"advantage\" would be that we spare the extra redundant VMWRITE for setting EPTP before running the vCPU first time, but the disadvantage would be more complicated logic.)\n\nBTW could you point at where is that statement that no VMWRITE to required VMCS field will result in unpredictable behavior? (I find it not easy to navigate in the SDM. :))",
      "parentUuid": "48758e5c_56de6fa6",
      "revId": "f393bebdd3097b25b477075b23e06795659a2efc",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1e35561b_4375c040",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/pkvm.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 1645,
      "author": {
        "id": 4085392
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-05-09T10:34:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "You probably need an up-to-date SDM. Mine is 2026 Feb. Vol 3C, section 27.11.3 Initializing a VMCS\n```\nSoftware should initialize fields in a VMCS (using VMWRITE) before using the VMCS for VM entry. Failure to do so\nmay result in unpredictable behavior; for example, a VM entry may fail for unexplained reasons, or a successful\ntransition (VM entry or VM exit) may load processor state with unexpected values.\n```",
      "parentUuid": "2b498279_998e3f68",
      "revId": "f393bebdd3097b25b477075b23e06795659a2efc",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
